WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.754
A workshop dashboard polls every ten seconds for a
changed machine status.

00:00:04.754 --> 00:00:10.729
With changes occurring at arbitrary times, the
average wait until the next poll is about five

00:00:10.729 --> 00:00:12.913
seconds, ignoring request latency.

00:00:12.913 --> 00:00:18.888
Reducing the interval lowers that wait but increases
the number of requests even when nothing

00:00:18.888 --> 00:00:19.402
changes.

00:00:19.402 --> 00:00:25.535
A WebSocket keeps a bidirectional channel open,
while long polling lets the server hold a request

00:00:25.535 --> 00:00:28.191
until data or a timeout becomes available.

00:00:28.191 --> 00:00:31.289
The connection does not eliminate state recovery.

00:00:31.289 --> 00:00:37.611
If a browser loses its network for thirty seconds,
it may miss several transitions regardless of the

00:00:37.611 --> 00:00:39.002
chosen live transport.

00:00:39.002 --> 00:00:45.094
On reconnect, retrieve the current authoritative
status or resume from a position supported by the

00:00:45.094 --> 00:00:46.399
application protocol.

00:00:46.399 --> 00:00:52.616
Distinguish a dashboard that needs only the latest
temperature from an audit screen that needs every

00:00:52.616 --> 00:00:53.299
transition.

00:00:53.299 --> 00:00:57.153
That requirement determines how much history must be
retained.

00:00:57.153 --> 00:01:03.307
Compare latency, idle connection resources and
reconnect behaviour, rather than choosing a protocol

00:01:03.307 --> 00:01:06.042
solely because it is described as real time.

00:01:06.042 --> 00:01:11.995
An account has two browser tabs, one connected to
notification worker A and another to worker B.

00:01:11.995 --> 00:01:15.778
An appointment service running elsewhere generates
an update.

00:01:15.778 --> 00:01:21.793
Sending the update to an arbitrary notification
worker is insufficient: only the worker holding a

00:01:21.793 --> 00:01:24.459
particular live connection can write to it.

00:01:24.459 --> 00:01:30.350
A registration directory maps the logical recipient
to connection identifiers and their owners,

00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:33.699
allowing the notification layer to route to both
tabs.

00:01:33.742 --> 00:01:36.669
Close one tab just after the directory lookup.

00:01:36.669 --> 00:01:42.776
The route can become stale between lookup and send,
so lookup success cannot guarantee delivery.

00:01:42.776 --> 00:01:48.374
Clean up failed destinations and let reconnecting
clients establish fresh registrations.

00:01:48.374 --> 00:01:54.290
Keep transport failures separate from the underlying
appointment change, which remains stored

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:55.181
independently.

00:01:55.181 --> 00:02:01.479
If the design promises recovery of missed updates,
retain an appropriate event history or provide a

00:02:01.479 --> 00:02:02.560
fresh-state read.

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:08.054
Trace a worker restart as well as a normal send;
this reveals why connection ownership,

00:02:08.054 --> 00:02:12.727
authoritative data and notification progress are
different kinds of state.
